Nvidia shield tablet may well be an  early glimpse at the future of gaming  the one device to rule them all idea  that lets you surf and email and work  and game from a tablet on the go and  then kick back with a controller and  playing your big-screen TV when you're  at home it's absolutely a neat concept  but I'm not so sure the tech is quite  there just yet to pull it off that tech  is based on NVIDIA Tegra k1  system-on-a-chip which uses a Kepler  based GPU with 192 cuda cores paired  with a quad-core arm cortex a15 2  gigabytes of RAM and a sharp 190 200 by  1200 pixel inch display it's an  impressive setup which makes for  impressive performance across the board  shield tablet zips through pretty much  anything you throw at it which makes  navigating through the largely stock  version of Android 4.4.3 it ships with  and absolute joy some might be  disappointed not to see a higher dpi  display here such as that of the iPad  Mini that shield tablets display is  clear bright and has great viewing  angles it's also better for running  games the more taxing of which will  stress the GPU at 1080p nvm anything  higher that display also makes a lot  more sense when you see the price tag at  just $299 or 229 pounds for the 16  gigabyte Wi-Fi version or $399 or 299  pounds for a 32 gigabyte version with 4G  the shield tablet is certainly in the  ballpark of the lights of the Nexus 7  and I'd more than happily pay the extra  few pounds for the extra power and  features on offer for starters there's  the wonderful inclusion of a microSD  slot for up to 128 gigabytes of storage  expansion which will prove particularly  useful if you got a large game and media  collection the stereo speakers to either  side of the screen are plenty loud  there's an actual mini HDMI slot for  video out which saves faffing with micro  USB adapters  you're banned 802 11 n Wi-Fi and there's  a stylus because hey styluses yay  battery life is good if not stunning  Nvidia claims ten hours of video  playback and I could certainly squeeze  out a day's worth of heavy use before  having to reach for a charger playing  games does zap the battery fast though  so if you're planning on playing lots of  games you will need to keep the charge  as a hand there are also 5 megapixel  cameras on the front and back of the  device the rear camera doesn't take the  best pictures for the front-facing  camera makes for surprisingly good  selfie cam the stills and video indeed  even if you never use any of the shield  tablets gaming specific features you're  getting a great tablet for the money it  feels solidly built with a reassuring  390 gram weight that's not too heavy for  one-handed use and a reasonable  thickness of 9.2 millimeters and while  there's no accounting for taste  I'd argue that it looks pretty good too  if you do use the gaming features you'll  need to fork out for the shield  controller for an extra fifty nine  dollars for 49 pounds and the kickstand  case spoon of the 39 bucks there are no  discounted bundles at launch which is a  missed opportunity for the full gaming  setup you're looking at almost an extra  $100 on top of the tablet price and for  that the shield controller is a bit of  an ugly duckling and is on the large  side so those with smaller hands might  find it a little uncomfortable the d-pad  and shoulder buttons well they're a tad  mushy too that said it does work as  advertised although I found it all too  easy to accidentally activate the touch  sensitive Android buttons while playing  a game the controller comes into its own  when you plug the tablet into a TV via  HDMI and it offers to start in console  mode and it brings up a simple to the  point interface that lets you load up  apps and games purchase them on the  native games front there on a whole  at launch so far there are just 11 Tegra  k1 optimized games including half-life 2  the tablet also comes with a free copy  of trine 2 which is a nice touch I tried  both half-life 2 and trying to and found  both games look great but didn't run  without issue half-life 2 in particular  had lots of noticeable framerate issues  during busiest scenes and while it  didn't ever get to the point where the  game was unplayable who was  disappointing to see from a Tegra k1  device twitch streaming is native to the  shield tablet and is easily activated at  any point by holding down the back  button on the controller for the most  part it works well complete with picture  in picture and audio from the built-in  mic but the framerate in native android  games takes a hit when the feature is  activated perhaps an update or two will  fix this in the future but for now  graphics intensive games and twitch  streaming aren't the best of friends  fortunately twitch streaming and game  stream do work well together game stream  lets you stream games from your PC to  your tablet wirelessly and shrink aims  do look impressive provided that you've  got a good clear wireless signal while  there's no mistaking them from native  games thanks to a few lights video  compression artifacts and look and play  great however if you've got a poor Wi-Fi  connection then the quality and  responsiveness of stream games takes a  hit I like to think of the shield tablet  this way if you buy one you're getting a  great powerful Android tablet with an  ear stock version of the OS with some  cool gaming features thrown in for a  great price  I wouldn't necessarily get a shield  tablet for its gaming features alone  though indeed if you're buying it solely  for the gaming features you might be a  little disappointed but if you're  interested in what the future of gaming  might look like shield tablet is perhaps  the best glimpse we've had at it so far  Oh
 

